<rml>
<head>
    <link type="text/rcss" href="theme.rcss"/>
    <link type="text/rcss" href="sidebar_hud.rcss"/>
</head>
<body data-model="sidebar_hud">
    <!-- ==================================================================
         TERMINAL PHOSPHOR SIDEBAR HUD
         ==================================================================

         THIS DOCUMENT IS A TERMINAL, NOT A SET OF BOXES.

         The HUD is a character-cell grid: one font, one size, one weight, one
         line-height, and every region a whole number of cells in both axes.
         That is a discipline rather than a style. Because nothing straddles a
         cell, columns authored by two different producers still line up, a
         frame drawn as text still closes at its corners, and a producer cannot
         emit a string that silently shifts everything to its right by half a
         character. `hud_phosphor::layout_for` snaps every rect to the grid;
         `hud_phosphor::pad` guarantees each row is exactly its region's width.

         The consequence for this file is that there is no structural markup to
         write. Every frame stroke — `─ │ ┌ ┐ └ ┘ ├ ┤ ┬ ┴ ┼` — is a real
         Unicode box-drawing glyph inside a text run emitted by a producer, so
         junctions can be the correct member of the set and a section title can
         interrupt its own rule (`──┤ SOMA ├──`) for zero extra rows. A CSS
         border cannot do either: it cannot pick a junction glyph and it cannot
         be interrupted. There is therefore not one `border` property anywhere
         in the phosphor stylesheet, and nothing here nests a wrapper whose only
         job would be to carry one. Each region is a positioned div holding one
         bound child, and THAT CHILD IS DELIBERATELY CLASS-LESS — a plain
         `<div data-rml="…">`. The producer emits its own row elements: one
         `.ph-row` per text row. It has to be that way round, because only
         the producer knows its row count — the message log emits a variable
         number of rows, and each needs its own `id="log-N"` for `hud_anim` to
         target — and it is uniform across all nine bindings so that nobody has
         to remember which fields arrive pre-wrapped and which do not. Do not
         "helpfully" add a `ph-*` class to a bound element here: `data-rml` sets
         INNER RML, so a classed container would nest a one-cell-tall
         `white-space: pre` box inside another one and clip it — which presents
         as a font or layout bug a long way from its actual cause.

         WHY EXACTLY TWO REGIONS ARE OPAQUE.

         The register spends its entire colour budget on one hue, so every
         distinction on screen — chrome vs. datum, intact vs. damaged, old log
         line vs. new — is carried by luminance alone. A luminance-only encoding
         is only readable over a background whose luminance is controlled: lay
         the same seven-rung ladder over daylight grass and then over a night
         basement and the middle rungs swap places against their ground. So the
         two regions that hold state a player must never misread carry an opaque
         ground: `#hud-status` (safe mode, hostiles, stats, needs) and
         `#hud-keys` (which action is on which key, and which are unavailable —
         encoded as a luminance step down to `ink::dead`, which is precisely the
         distinction an uncontrolled backdrop would eat).

         Everything else — `#hud-soma`, `#hud-dock`, `#hud-log`,
         `#hud-vehicle` — floats over live terrain behind `.ph-veil`: a
         translucent amber-black ground plus a `backdrop-filter` that blurs and
         desaturates what is underneath, borrowed from design 10. The blur is
         not decoration; it is what makes the backdrop's luminance locally
         uniform enough for the ladder to hold, and the desaturation is what
         keeps a green field or a red fire from reading as a second hue in a
         single-hue interface.

         AND WHY THAT IS AN OCCLUSION SAVING, NOT A COSMETIC CHOICE.

         Floating means floating: the four veiled panels are drawn on top of the
         map, they are NOT carved out of the terrain viewport. Only the two
         opaque strips are (`sidebar_hud_top_rows` / `_bottom_rows`), because
         only they hide what is under them. The shipping HUD reserved an
         edge-to-edge sidebar column and then spent 45% of that column's height
         on empty log trough; here the log is sized to its line count and the
         body/overmap panels cost the viewport nothing at all. The panels can be
         generous precisely because they are translucent and unreserved.

         TYPE METRICS ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT FROM THIS FILE AND FROM THE RCSS.

         `font-size` and `line-height` are written onto the document from C++ by
         `sidebar_hud_apply_rect`, out of `hud_phosphor::metrics`. They have to
         be: the cell size is derived from the context's dp dimensions so the
         grid tiles the viewport with no remainder, and RCSS cannot see those
         dimensions. Anything that sets a font size here or in the stylesheet
         would desynchronise the glyphs from the geometry that was computed for
         them — box stems would stop abutting between rows and every region
         would drift from its snapped rect. If text looks wrong, the bug is in
         `metrics_for`, not in this document.

         TREE ORDER IS PAINT ORDER. The three overlays come last so the CRT
         glass and the damage flash sit over the HUD as well as over the map. -->

    <!-- STATUS — full width, top edge, opaque. Two rows of world and character
         state, then the rule.

         The rule is the THIRD ROW OF THIS REGION, not a region of its own, and
         that is deliberate. It spans the whole viewport and breaks at the same
         two columns where the SOMA and DOCK panels below it have their
         verticals. `hud_status_rule` receives the whole `hud_phosphor::layout`
         rather than a bare width for exactly that reason: it reads the panels'
         own column stops off it, drops a `┼` at each, and stamps BOTH section
         titles — `┤ SOMA ├` and `┤ OVERMAP ├` — into this one row. Read
         together, the strip is the header row of the panels hanging beneath it
         and the three objects form one continuous L of frame; split into its own
         element it would read as an unrelated horizontal line that happens to be
         near them. The load-bearing consequence is that SOMA and DOCK draw
         neither a top edge nor a title of their own — their first emitted row is
         content — so one screen-wide stroke does the work of three that would
         otherwise have to be kept in agreement. -->
    <div id="hud-status">
        <div data-rml="status_row1_rml"></div>
        <div data-rml="status_row2_rml"></div>
        <div data-rml="status_rule_rml"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- SOMA — left column under the status rule, translucent. Body parts with
         their bars, the pools, and the per-part effect state. Left-hand because
         a critical limb is the first thing a left-to-right reader must hit, and
         its effect words sit on the row directly under the bar they describe
         rather than in a strip at the far side of the screen. Its title and its
         top edge belong to the status rule above; the sub-rules it does own
         (`POOLS`, `EFFECTS`) terminate in `┤`, because this panel's only
         vertical is on its right — the side facing the play area. -->
    <div id="hud-soma" class="ph-veil">
        <div data-rml="soma_rml"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- RADAR — right column under the status rule, and the one region that is
         NOT veiled. Its content is the dot minimap, drawn as GPU quads on the UI
         compositing layer, which sits UNDER this document; a translucent veil over
         it would multiply the two dimmest rungs of the ladder into the ground and
         delete every open-terrain dot. `hud_radar::draw` therefore paints its own
         `ink::ground` fill first, and all this element contributes is the vertical
         that closes the panel's play-area-facing edge. Titled by the status rule
         above, as SOMA and DOCK are. -->
    <div id="hud-radar">
        <div data-rml="radar_rml"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- DOCK — right column under the RADAR region, translucent. Mission marker,
         target, arms. Mirrors to the left column with SIDEBAR_POSITION, which
         is handled by `layout_for`'s `sidebar_right`; this document holds no
         side-specific markup. Titled by the status rule, as SOMA is, and
         mirrored in frame as well as in position: its sub-rules (`TARGET`,
         `ARMS`) open with `├` because its vertical is on its left. Each panel
         draws only the edge that faces the play area, because the screen edge is
         already the fourth wall and a second stroke inside the bezel would be a
         redundant frame. -->
    <div id="hud-dock" class="ph-veil">
        <div data-rml="dock_rml"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- LOG — bottom-left above the keys strip, translucent, and sized to
         exactly the number of message rows it is showing. No scroller and no
         well: a fixed-height trough is what let the shipping HUD hold 267 dp of
         message in 752 dp of reserved column. Recency is the luminance ramp
         across the rows, so the newest line is the brightest thing here and
         needs no separate highlight. Unlike SOMA and DOCK this panel does draw
         its own titled top rule as its first row — it does not touch the status
         strip, so it has nothing above it to borrow a frame from. -->
    <div id="hud-log" class="ph-veil">
        <div data-rml="log_rml"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- KEYS — full width, bottom edge, opaque. The DOS function-key line,
         where DOS put it.

         `keys_rule_rml` is the FIRST ROW OF THIS REGION and it is also the
         bottom edge of the log panel above — one row doing two jobs. That is
         why `hud_keys_rule` reads the log's rect off the layout: it drops a `┴`
         at each of the log's two column stops — the counterparts of the `┬` and
         the `┐` in the log's own top rule — while continuing as a plain
         rule to both screen edges. Giving the log its own closing row and this
         strip its own opening row would cost a screen-wide row to draw the same
         line twice, one cell apart, and the two lines would then disagree at
         any column where the log's frame did not line up. Keeping the rule here
         rather than in `#hud-log` also keeps it on the opaque ground, so the
         one stroke that touches both regions has a single, stable colour. -->
    <div id="hud-keys">
        <div data-rml="keys_rule_rml"></div>
        <div data-rml="keys_rml"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- VEHICLE — right column under the dock, translucent. `display: none`
         unless the avatar is controlling a vehicle; the chassis toggles it. -->
    <div id="hud-vehicle" class="ph-veil">
        <div data-rml="veh_rml"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- CRT scanline glass — full-screen, no content. A repeating gradient at
         cell-locked pitch, weak enough (mean ~2.5% darkening) that the tilemap
         stays crisp. Its job is to make the HUD and the world share one
         emissive surface instead of looking like text pasted onto a game. -->
    <div id="hud-crt-scan"></div>

    <!-- CRT faceplate vignette — full-screen, no content. Radial darkening
         towards the corners, the honest half of a curved-glass tube: barrel
         distortion needs a shader, corner falloff does not. Above the scanline
         layer so the falloff dims the scanlines too, which is what a real
         faceplate does. -->
    <div id="hud-crt-curve"></div>

    <!-- Damage flash — full-screen, no content. Pre-existing behaviour, driven
         by `hud_anim` on total-HP loss. Last of all, so a hit is legible over
         the CRT glass; it is the one element in the HUD permitted a hue,
         because it is an event and not a reading. -->
    <div id="hud-vignette"></div>
</body>
</rml>
